1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,280
One of the largest battleships in Europe was stopped by a slow, fabric-covered biplane.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,900
Her name was Bismarck.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,730
In May 1941 she set out on her first and only combat sortie.

4
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:15,300
In the Denmark Strait she met the British battlecruiser Hood.

5
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:18,120
After eight minutes of firing, the Hood blew up.

6
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:21,160
Of more than 1,400 men, three survived.

7
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:22,820
But the Bismarck had been hit as well.

8
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:27,880
Fuel leaked from a hole in her bow, and Admiral Lütjens set course for France.

9
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:31,090
The Royal Navy then hunted her with dozens of ships.

10
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:36,560
Two days later, 15 Swordfish torpedo bombers from the carrier Ark Royal attacked.

11
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:39,440
One torpedo hit the stern and jammed the rudder.

12
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:43,420
The Bismarck could only sail in circles and could no longer escape her pursuers.

13
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:47,700
The next morning the battleships King George V and Rodney arrived.

14
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:51,680
A hit destroyed the bridge; the ship's command was probably killed.

15
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:56,410
The executive officer had the ship scuttled so the crew could abandon her.

16
00:00:56,430 --> 00:01:00,170
Of more than 2,200 men, 114 survived.

17
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:07,350
Almost fifty years later her wreck was found by Robert Ballard, who had also discovered the Titanic, almost five kilometres down.
